{
  "title": "iOS: restore where you were after the OS terminates the app in the background",
  "description": "When iOS suspends and then kills Ramekin in the background (common while cooking: the phone locks, another app takes memory), relaunching drops you back on the recipe list with default state. \
Everything that says where you were lives in @State and is lost: ContentView.selectedTab and RecipesTab's NavigationPath in ramekin-ios/Ramekin/RamekinApp.swift, plus the recipe detail view model's scale and custom scale text (RecipeDetailViewModel), the scroll position, and any open sheet. \
Persist enough to come back to the same place: (1) the selected tab and the navigation stack (make NavigationDestination Codable and keep NavigationPath.codable in @SceneStorage, so it is per scene and cleared when the user swipes the app away, which is iOS's convention); (2) per open recipe, the scale and the scroll position (the ingredient or step in view); (3) for in-progress edits, the RecipeFormView draft, so a half-typed edit isn't lost (store the draft, not the sheet, and offer to resume it); (4) the meal plan week and shopping list scroll if cheap. \
Restoring must tolerate a recipe that was deleted or changed by sync meanwhile (fall back to the list) and must not block launch on the network: it should restore from the local cache (RecipeCacheStore) first. \
Test it with the simulator flow in the iOS repro notes: background the app, terminate it from Xcode (which keeps scene storage, unlike swiping it away), relaunch, and add a UI test for at least tab + open recipe + scale. \
Web already keeps your place through the URL (routes like /recipes/:id, and the scale as ?scale= in ViewRecipePage), so this is iOS only.",
